<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gongsun Yan</h1><p class="st-meta">189 CE – 238 CE · capital Xiangping</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A semi-independent regime in Liaodong that held the north-east for three generations and stretched into Korea, until Sima Yi destroyed it in 238.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 189, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> disintegrated, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-du">Gongsun Du</a> was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> and quickly made himself its independent master, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Xiangping</a> (Liaoyang). He expanded against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wuhuan">Wuhuan</a> and controlled the Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-kang">Gongsun Kang</a> created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daifang-commandery">Daifang Commandery</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a> around 204 and in 207 sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> the heads of the fugitive Yuan brothers, buying recognition. The regime controlled trade routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-yuan">Gongsun Yuan</a>, who seized power in 228, courted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-wu">Eastern Wu</a>, then killed its envoys to please <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>, and in 237 proclaimed himself King of Yan.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 238 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a>, who besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Xiangping</a>, captured and executed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-yuan">Gongsun Yuan</a> and massacred thousands of men. Wei took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daifang-commandery">Daifang</a>, opening direct contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wa-japan">Wa of Japan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gongsun-yan.jpg" alt="Gongsun Yan" width="320" height="304"><figcaption>Image: Three Kingdoms fan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Kingdoms_timelapse.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>189 CE – 238 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xiangping</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gongsun Du, Gongsun Kang, Gongsun Yuan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liaodong Gongsun regime, Yan (Gongsun Yuan)</dd></dl></aside></div>

